Z||000000||bilbo||02-05-2001||03:43 PM||||I JUST GOT A SET OF BERETTA GTZ WHEELS AND THEY DON'T HAVE A MARK ON THEM EXCEPT FOR CLEAR COAT PEELING.I WANT TO POLISH THEM UP THOUGH,MY QUESTION IS CAN I JUST USE 1000 GRIT SANDPAPER AND POLISHING COMPOUND OR DO I HAVE TO START WITH 250-500-1000-1500 GRIT AND GO THROUGH ALL THE OTHER STEPS???????||||reg||1
Z||000001||b lo 0||02-06-2001||04:21 PM||||First off, take a. acid-based polished wheel cleaner to the entire wheel surface.  This will take off all the clearcoat and paint and even smooth out the surface a bit.  Then get some aluminum polishing compund and a buffing wheel on a high torque drill and buff the sucker.  When done, clean with Windex.  You may have to use a LOT of elbow grease with a soft cloth where the buffer won't go.  I've done this before, but the wheels were a LOT simpler in design than GTZ wheels.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT (in progress)  <BR>titled ICE-plates B LO 0 <BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery/ice.gif">     <BR>Go to <A HREF="http://www.audioimagery.i-p.com" TARGET=_blank>www.audioimagery.i-p.com</A>  !!!<P><A HREF="http://clubs.excite.com/welcome/main?cid=.zXb1DJ1lsCL&aid=22&auth=" TARGET=_blank>Pontiac Fiero Owner's Club</A>-Pics, Videos and More-Or Post your Own||||reg||1
